EXT. ROPE FERRY ON THE SABINE RIVER - NOON

A flat-bottomed ferry on a hawser strung across a brown, slow river. Cottonwoods on either bank. The sun beats on warped planks. A rusted bell hangs from a post.

AMOS "OLD HICKORY" TREVELYAN-BELL, 60s, ferryman, hauls the rope hand over hand, forearms like knotted cord. His hat is pulled low.

On the far end of the deck, leaning against a barrel with a rifle across his knees, sits JEDEDIAH "JED" CORVALLIS-ODUYA, 30s, in a duster gone grey with trail dust. He has not spoken since they pushed off.

AMOS
Fare was two bits, Jed.

JED
I paid it.

AMOS
You paid it in Mexican coin.

JED
Silver's silver.

AMOS
Not if the marshal says otherwise.

Jed shifts the rifle. The barrel catches the sun. Amos does not look at it. He hauls. The ferry creeps forward, water slapping the hull.

AMOS (CONT'D)
You're a long way from Fort Smith.

JED
So I'm told.

AMOS
They say a man shot the sheriff there last Thursday.

JED
They say a lot.

AMOS
They say he had a scar on his lip.

Jed touches his lip. A thin white line, old. He lowers the hand slowly.

JED
Lots of men have scars, Hickory.

AMOS
Lots do.

Amos spits over the rail. Wipes his palm on his trousers. Reaches for the rope again, and as he does so, his other hand drifts toward a short-handled axe hung from a peg on the post, and rests there, easy, like a man steadying himself on a rail.

Jed watches the hand. Jed's thumb finds the hammer of the rifle. Neither of them moves. A heron lifts from the reeds, unfolds, and passes across the sun.

JED (CONT'D)
That axe for the rope?

AMOS
For the rope.

JED
Rope looks fine.

AMOS
It's a precaution.

JED
Against what?

AMOS
Against men who ask a lot of questions about axes.

Jed almost smiles. Thumb drops from the hammer. He rests the rifle on his knees again, barrel to the sky.

JED
I never shot a sheriff, Hickory.

AMOS
I never said you did.

JED
You said a man with a scar.

AMOS
I said a lot of things.

JED
I shot his deputy.

The bell on the post tolls once, nudged by a gust. Amos' hand stops on the axe.

AMOS
That a confession?

JED
It's a weather report.

AMOS
Deputy a good man?

JED
He had a wife in Muskogee. He told me so, twice, before it started.

AMOS
That doesn't answer me.

JED
No. It doesn't.

The ferry nears midstream. The current tugs the hull. The rope hums. Amos lets it run slack, and the deck tilts slowly with the river.

AMOS
You know I'd be in my rights to put you in the water.

JED
You would.

AMOS
You know I'd be in my rights to take that rifle.

JED
You'd be in your rights to try.

Amos unhooks the axe. Holds it by the head, handle down, as if offering it. Jed looks at the handle. At Amos' eyes. He does not take it.

AMOS
Four hundred dollars, Jed. That's the paper on you.

JED
I know the figure.

AMOS
My boy's in the territorial prison. Four hundred dollars would buy him a lawyer.

JED
Then you'd best cut the rope and let the river carry me back across.

AMOS
And if I don't?

JED
Then we both get to the far bank, and you forget my coin was Mexican.

Silence. The cottonwoods hiss. The ferry drifts. Amos stares at the water, at the axe, at the bank still thirty yards off.

He hangs the axe back on its peg.

AMOS
(quiet)
Silver's silver.

He bends to the rope and hauls, and the ferry begins to move again, slow and steady, toward the far shore.

JED
Thank you, Hickory.

AMOS
Don't thank me. Pull the rope.

Jed lays the rifle across the barrel, stands, and takes hold of the line behind Amos. They haul together. The bell clangs with each pull, the sun hangs straight overhead, and the bank slides slowly nearer.

JED
Where's your boy held?

AMOS
Huntsville.

JED
I'll send a letter. To a man I know.

AMOS
Don't promise what you can't do.

JED
I never do.

The ferry bumps the landing. Mud sucks at the hull. Jed steps over the rail, onto the bank. He looks back once. Amos has already turned the ferry's nose toward the other side.

JED (CONT'D)
So long, Hickory.

AMOS
Go on, Jed. Before I change my mind.

Jed walks up the bank into the cottonwoods. Amos hauls the rope home, alone.
